C. Hidradenitis suppurativa / acne inversa

This is chronic follicular occlusive disease of apocrine-bearing intertriginous skin with the classic tri-site distribution rather than a primary eczematous, allergic or psoriasiform process.

Involvement is simultaneous and symmetric in the three classic apocrine zones:
* axillary folds with large fluctuant / bulla-like lesions – in HS those “bullae” are tense inflammatory cysts / ruptured deep follicular abscesses with overlying maceration and friction-induced blistering, not true vesico-bullous disease;
* inframammary folds with erythematous edematous plaques;
* inguinal regions with painful purulent inflammatory nodules/abscesses.

That axillae – inframammary – groin triad with nodule → fluctuant abscess/pseudocyst → purulent drainage → early sinus tract formation and secondary maceration/scarring is the Hurley stage II-III phenotype of hidradenitis suppurativa.

The host profile is the established HS severity profile:
* BMI 39 obesity with obstructive sleep apnea and mechanical friction/moisture trapping in folds;
* Type 2 diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, peripheral artery disease – metabolic syndrome;
* Active tobacco use 2-3 cigarettes/day x 15 y;
* Female sex with inframammary predominance and multiparity.

Obesity, smoking and metabolic syndrome/diabetes are the strongest modifiable risk factors for onset and progression of acne inversa and explain chronic relapsing suppurative disease with secondary bacterial colonization and purulent discharge.

Allergic contact dermatitis, atopic dermatitis and intertriginous psoriasis do not give deep painful purulent nodulo-abscess-sinus disease confined to three apocrine sites; inverse psoriasis is well demarcated finely scaly without frank purulent nodules, fluctuant abscesses and sinus tracts.

Malignant intertrigo / intertrigo malignum / malignant pustulosis is the historic eponym for severe intertriginous suppurative disease and in modern nosology is classified as hidradenitis suppurativa/acne inversa. The severe bullous/erosive intertrigo described in elderly, diabetic, immunosuppressed hosts is the advanced inflammatory phenotype of HS. 

The prior ductal carcinoma and cyclophosphamide immunosuppression increase secondary infection risk, flare severity and wound healing impairment and make biopsy prudent to exclude superimposed infection or malignant infiltration in this immunocompromised host, but they do not change the underlying chronic follicular occlusive nodulo-abscess-sinus pattern in axillae, inframammary and inguinal folds. The most parsimonious and clinically consistent diagnosis is hidradenitis suppurativa, acne inversa, Hurley II-III.

